<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Test-only instrumentation manifest for the offline differential parser tests.
  GENERATED by tests/fixtures/generate_differential_manifest.py — do not hand-edit;
  regenerate, so every type keeps the same set of shapes.

  Never installed with `wevtutil im`. The test process calls TdhLoadManifest() on
  this file, which registers the schema for that process only and needs no
  elevation, so the tests run as ordinary unit tests.

  `Sentinel` is a fixed magic (0x5A5AA5A5) asserted on every fixture, so an over- or
  under-read corrupts it rather than landing silently in an unasserted field. `Lead`
  pushes the subject to an odd offset, so a parser assuming natural alignment fails.

  Each type appears in seven shapes, each varying one axis from a baseline.

  Offset axis — where the subject sits, and hence which predecessors it must fold:

    <Type>First       Subject | Sentinel
                      Offset zero, naturally aligned, no predecessor at all.
    <Type>Fixed       Lead | Subject | Sentinel
                      Constant but odd offset.
    <Type>Dynamic     Lead | Pad(UnicodeString) | Subject | Sentinel
                      Offset depends on Pad's run-time length, so no precomputed
                      constant satisfies it.
    <Type>Last        Lead | Sentinel | Subject
                      Nothing follows. A parser that runs a trailing field to the end
                      of the buffer regardless of its declared length passes the other
                      three and fails only this one.

  Multiplicity axis — element striding is its own computation, and differs between
  fixed-size elements (constant stride) and variable-size ones (re-scan per element):

    <Type>ArrayFixed        Lead | Subject[3] | Sentinel
    <Type>ArrayParamCount   Lead | SubjectCount | Subject[SubjectCount] | Sentinel
                            Also carries the count == 0 case, from the fixture side.

  Container axis — struct members are reached through struct_start_index /
  struct_member_count, a different walk from the top-level one:

    <Type>StructMember      Lead | { MemberU32, Subject } | Sentinel
-->
